diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) unable to reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) due to spine_router_0_0 — is not confirmed by any live evidence.

**Phase 1** reachability probing showed 0% packet loss in both directions (pc_0_0→pc_0_1 and reverse), with sub-2 ms RTTs, ruling out a current data-plane failure.

**Phase A (L2/infrastructure):** infra_sweep scanned all 7 devices and raised zero flags — no ACL drops, no ARP anomalies, all hosts have correct IPs and default routes, no resolver divergence. l2_snapshot flagged only the all-zeros MACs on kernel tunnel pseudo-interfaces (tunl0, gre0, etc.), which are present and DOWN on every Linux host by default; this is baseline, not a conflict.

**Phase B (control plane):** bgp_snapshot confirmed all five FRR routers (both leaves, both spines, super-spine) have watchfrr/zebra/bgpd active and all BGP sessions Established. Advertisements are correct (leaf_router_0_0 originates 10.0.0.0/24, leaf_router_0_1 originates 10.0.1.0/24). tc_snapshot found no non-default qdiscs or shaping rules on any of the 16 inspected interfaces.

**Phase D (resources/services):** pressure_sweep returned zero suspect devices across all 7 nodes — no CPU spikes, no socket exhaustion, no stress tooling.

**Root cause:** No fault is present. The network is fully operational. The ticket's claim that spine_router_0_0 "went wrong in the night" is unconfirmed — there is no flap script, no carrier anomaly, no BGP session history indicating a past disruption, and no current mechanism producing packet loss.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed; the fabric is healthy. Advise the user to retest from their own machine and verify their local routing/gateway configuration.
=== END ===